%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level5_MacLab
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_5
  levelNumber: 5
  displayName: The Compromised Campus Network
  sceneName: Room5_MacLab
  description: Keeping evidence safe, the hidden checks inside an email, identity-theft
    forms, ransomware, fake QR codes and the right way to report.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: ec2bc026c46543c4788ea2715af04007, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 5: THE COMPROMISED CAMPUS NETWORK'
  briefingText: 'In this room: keeping evidence safe, the hidden checks inside an
    email, identity-theft forms, ransomware, fake QR codes and the right way to report.

    You''ll
    get 6 questions: 4 with answer choices (A-D) and 2 to judge as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
    Take your time and read each one carefully.'
  caseNotes:
  - A "Scholarship Grant 2027" form is shared in a student group. It uses the school
    logo.
  - The canteen's "Scan to pay with PayLite" poster looks normal, but up close the
    QR code is a sticker placed on top of the printed one.
  objectives:
  - Answer all 6 questions.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose. Stuck? Press HINT.
  - Score 870+ to finish the investigation.
  messages:
  - id: H05
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: A friend was scammed through a chat app and wants to file a report. <b>Which
      way of handling the evidence is best?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Investigators can only trust evidence that is <b>complete</b> and
      <b>unchanged</b>. Cropping hides context, and blurring hides the scammer's
      name they need. Keeping the original and noting when you saved each item is
      called the <b>chain of custody</b>. Uninstalling may delete the chat.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: chat
      field: 5
      match: untouched on the original phone
      label: Original chat
      finding: Complete, unchanged evidence on the original device can be trusted.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: complete
      part: chat
    - id: shots
      field: 5
      match: profile and number, with dates and times
      label: Full screenshots
      finding: Identifiers and timestamps are what investigators need to trace the
        scammer.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: ids
      part: shots
    - id: receipt
      field: 5
      match: reference numbers
      label: Receipts
      finding: Reference numbers can help trace the money.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: ids
      part: receipt
    - id: crop
      field: 5
      match: cropped screenshot with the scammer's name blurred
      label: Edited copy
      finding: Cropping removes context and blurring hides identifiers. Edited evidence
        is weaker evidence.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: edited
      part: crop
    - id: log
      field: 5
      match: when and how each item was collected
      label: Collection note
      finding: 'This is a basic chain of custody: it shows the evidence wasn''t changed.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: custody
      part: log
    evidence:
    - id: complete
      title: Complete and unchanged
      whyItMatters: Evidence has to be complete to be trusted.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: ids
      title: Identifiers kept
      whyItMatters: Names, numbers and references are the leads.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: edited
      title: Edits weaken evidence
      whyItMatters: Don't crop or blur; share copies instead.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: custody
      title: Chain of custody
      whyItMatters: Record when and how each item was collected.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Crop the screenshots to just the scam message and blur the scammer's name for
      privacy
    - Forward everything to a big group chat so more people can confirm it
    - Keep the original chat untouched, take full screenshots (with names, dates
      and times), save receipts, share only copies, and note when you saved each
      one
    - Uninstall the app to stop the scammer and write down what you remember
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: Keep the <b>originals untouched</b>, share copies, and note when you
      saved each one.
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Legal Aspects of Cyber Forensics
      (\"maintaining evidence integrity\"). Supporting: Digital Evidence and Evidence
      Collection"
    hint: Investigators need evidence that is complete and unchanged.
    activity:
      kind: 9
      title: Your friend's evidence folder
      instruction: Check each item. Which evidence can investigators trust?
      items:
      - key: chat
        role: 6
        label: original
        text: Full chat with the scammer, untouched on the original phone
      - key: shots
        role: 6
        label: screenshots
        text: Full screenshots showing the profile and number, with dates and times
      - key: receipt
        role: 6
        label: receipts
        text: Payment receipts with reference numbers
      - key: crop
        role: 6
        label: edited copy
        text: A cropped screenshot with the scammer's name blurred
      - key: log
        role: 8
        label: 
        text: A note of when and how each item was collected
      minClues: 2
      outcomes:
      - Investigators can't confirm the cropped images - and the blurred name was
        the lead they needed.
      - Your friend's personal data spreads through the group, and copies get changed
        along the way.
      - Investigators get complete, unchanged evidence with a clear record of how
        it was collected.
      - Uninstalling may wipe the chat history, leaving the report to rest on memory
        alone.
  - id: H06
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: 'Email header excerpt:

      From: "Registrar''s Office" <noparse><</noparse>registrar@uphsl-binan.example<noparse>></noparse>

      Reply-To:
      registrar.office.ph@freemail.example

      Return-Path: <noparse><</noparse>bounce@mailer-xyz.example<noparse>></noparse>

      Authentication-Results:
      spf=fail; dkim=none; dmarc=fail

      Subject: FINAL reminder: unpaid tuition,
      account on hold

      <b>Which clue best proves this email is fake (spoofed)?</b>'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'The "From" name can say anything. <b>SPF and DMARC</b> are checks
      that ask: did the school''s own mail system really send this? Both <b>failed</b>.
      Reply-To also sends your answer to the scammer. The subject and the time are
      only weak clues.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: display
      field: 5
      match: '"Registrar''s Office"'
      label: Display name
      finding: The From line can say anything. A display name proves nothing.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: from
    - id: spf
      field: 5
      match: spf=fail
      label: SPF
      finding: 'SPF fail: the registrar''s domain did not authorize the server that
        sent this email.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: authfail
      part: auth
    - id: dmarc
      field: 5
      match: dmarc=fail
      label: DMARC
      finding: 'DMARC fail: the email doesn''t line up with the registrar''s own
        domain policy.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: authfail
      part: auth
    - id: reply
      field: 5
      match: freemail.example
      label: Reply-To
      finding: Replies quietly go to a free-mail address - straight to the attacker.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: replyto
      part: reply
    - id: ret
      field: 5
      match: mailer-xyz.example
      label: Return-Path
      finding: A different bounce domain. Worth noting, but the authentication results
        tell you more.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: ret
    - id: subject
      field: 5
      match: FINAL reminder
      label: Subject
      finding: Pressure is a weak sign on its own. Real reminders can sound urgent
        too.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: subject
    - id: time
      field: 5
      match: 2:14 AM
      label: Time sent
      finding: Odd hours are a weak sign. Emails are sent at all hours.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: time
    evidence:
    - id: authfail
      title: SPF and DMARC fail
      whyItMatters: The domain didn't authorize the sender.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: replyto
      title: Reply-To goes to free mail
      whyItMatters: Your answer would go to the attacker.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - The sender checks (SPF and DMARC) fail, and replies go to a free-mail address
    - The subject mentions tuition
    - The display name says "Registrar's Office"
    - It was sent at 2:14 AM
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: SPF and DMARC <b>failed</b>, and replies go to free-mail. The "From"
      name is just text anyone can type.
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Network Forensics and Cybercrime
      Investigation (\"identify signs of cybercrimes\")"
    hint: The "From" name is just text. Which lines are checks the mail system actually
      ran?
    activity:
      kind: 1
      title: "Message source  \xB7  headers"
      instruction: Check each header line. Which one is strong proof?
      items:
      - key: from
        role: 0
        label: From
        text: '"Registrar''s Office" <registrar@uphsl-binan.example>'
      - key: reply
        role: 0
        label: Reply-To
        text: registrar.office.ph@freemail.example
      - key: ret
        role: 0
        label: Return-Path
        text: bounce@mailer-xyz.example
      - key: auth
        role: 0
        label: Authentication-Results
        text: spf=fail; dkim=none; dmarc=fail
      - key: subject
        role: 0
        label: Subject
        text: 'FINAL reminder: unpaid tuition, account on hold'
      - key: time
        role: 0
        label: Sent
        text: 2:14 AM
      minClues: 2
      outcomes: []
  - id: H07
    channel: 2
    sender: Scholarship Grant 2027 form
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scholarship Grant 2027
    body: 'Please provide: full name, student number, birthdate, mother''s maiden
      name, front and back of your ID, a selfie holding your ID, and your PayLite
      MPIN "for fast release of funds".


      There is no privacy notice. The only
      contact is a mobile number.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: A "Scholarship Grant 2027" form is shared in a student group.
      It uses the school logo.
    explanation: Real scholarships may ask for an ID, which makes this tricky. But
      <b>no one needs your MPIN to send you money</b>, and an ID plus a selfie holding
      it is all a scammer needs to open accounts as you. There is no privacy notice
      and only a mobile number, so check with the scholarship office directly.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sender
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: The form
      finding: Shared in a student group and using the school logo. Logos are easy
        to copy.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: 
    - id: maiden
      field: 3
      match: mother's maiden name
      label: It asks for
      finding: A classic security-question answer, used to reset accounts.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: secq
      part: 
    - id: selfie
      field: 3
      match: a selfie holding your ID
      label: It asks for
      finding: ID photos plus a selfie let someone open accounts as you.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: idtheft
      part: 
    - id: mpin
      field: 3
      match: your PayLite MPIN
      label: It asks for
      finding: Your MPIN is like your e-wallet password. Nobody legitimate asks for
        it.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: mpin
      part: 
    - id: notice
      field: 3
      match: There is no privacy notice.
      label: Privacy notice
      finding: No notice means no explanation of why the data is collected or what
        happens to it.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: notice
      part: 
    - id: contact
      field: 3
      match: The only contact is a mobile number.
      label: Contact
      finding: No office, no official address - nobody accountable.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: notice
      part: 
    evidence:
    - id: mpin
      title: Asks for your MPIN
      whyItMatters: Never share it with anyone.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: idtheft
      title: ID plus selfie
      whyItMatters: Everything needed to pretend to be you.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: secq
      title: Security-question answer
      whyItMatters: Used to take over accounts.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: notice
      title: No privacy notice, no real contact
      whyItMatters: Nobody is accountable for your data.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Scam! <b>No one needs your MPIN to pay you.</b> With your ID, selfie
      and MPIN, they can become you.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105) \u2014 Week 15: Data
      Privacy Act (RA 10173)"
    hint: Look at the PIN request. Does anyone need that to send you money?
    activity:
      kind: 0
      title: 
      instruction: 
      items: []
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: H08
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: After you open an email attachment on a lab PC, your files start changing
      to <b>.locked</b> and a note demands cryptocurrency. <b>What do you do first?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'First, stop it spreading: cut the network so it can''t reach other
      PCs. Leave the PC on so investigators keep the evidence. Paying doesn''t promise
      your files back, restarting can erase evidence, and a USB can carry the infection.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: locked
      field: 5
      match: .locked
      label: File names
      finding: Your files are being encrypted right now. This is ransomware.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: ransomware
      part: f1
    - id: locked2
      field: 5
      match: .locked
      label: More files
      finding: It's spreading through your files while you watch.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: ransomware
      part: f2
    - id: readme
      field: 5
      match: README_RESTORE_FILES.txt
      label: New file
      finding: The ransom note. Don't delete it - it's evidence.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: note
    - id: pay
      field: 5
      match: Pay in cryptocurrency
      label: The demand
      finding: Paying doesn't guarantee you'll get your files back.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: ransom
    - id: net
      field: 5
      match: shared drives
      label: Network
      finding: While connected, ransomware can spread to shared drives and other
        PCs. Cutting the connection contains it.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: contain
      part: net
    - id: power
      field: 5
      match: PC is on
      label: Power
      finding: Leaving it on keeps evidence in memory for investigators. Restarting
        can destroy it.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: evidence
      part: power
    evidence:
    - id: ransomware
      title: Files being encrypted
      whyItMatters: Ransomware is active on this PC.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: contain
      title: Still on the network
      whyItMatters: Disconnect first to stop it spreading.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: evidence
      title: Evidence in memory
      whyItMatters: Leave the PC on and report it.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Pay quickly before the price goes up
    - Unplug it from the network (or turn off Wi-Fi), leave it on, stop using it,
      and report to IT right away
    - Restart, run a cleanup tool and delete the ransom note
    - Copy your files to your USB drive to save them
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: <b>Disconnect, keep it on, report.</b> Stop the spread first. Investigators
      need the evidence.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Incident Response and Forensics"
    hint: First stop it from spreading, and keep the evidence.
    activity:
      kind: 8
      title: "Lab PC  \xB7  Documents"
      instruction: Check what's happening on this PC, then choose your first step.
      items:
      - key: f1
        role: 4
        label: changed just now
        text: Thesis_Draft.docx.locked
      - key: f2
        role: 4
        label: changed just now
        text: Budget.xlsx.locked
      - key: note
        role: 4
        label: new
        text: README_RESTORE_FILES.txt
      - key: ransom
        role: 5
        label: RANSOM NOTE
        text: Your files are encrypted. Pay in cryptocurrency to get them back.
      - key: net
        role: 6
        label: network
        text: "Connected  \xB7  lab network and shared drives"
      - key: power
        role: 6
        label: power
        text: PC is on
      minClues: 2
      outcomes:
      - The money goes to criminals with no guarantee your files come back - and
        the infection keeps spreading.
      - The infection is cut off from shared drives and other PCs, and the evidence
        in memory is kept for the incident team.
      - Restarting can destroy evidence in memory, and cleaning up doesn't stop what
        has already spread.
      - The USB copy holds already-locked files and may carry the infection to the
        next computer.
  - id: H09
    channel: 2
    sender: Page opened by the canteen QR code
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scan to pay with PayLite
    body: Enter your mobile number and MPIN to pay.
    link: paylite-merchant-pay.example/canteen
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: The canteen's "Scan to pay with PayLite" poster looks normal,
      but up close the QR code is a sticker placed on top of the printed one.
    explanation: This is <b>QR code phishing</b>. The sticker over the real code
      is the clue you can see. A real payment QR opens <b>inside your e-wallet app</b>
      and shows the shop's name. It never asks for your MPIN on a web page. Tell
      the canteen staff too.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sticker
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: How you got here
      finding: The QR code was a sticker placed on top of the printed one (case notes).
        Someone swapped it.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: sticker
      part: 
    - id: mpin
      field: 3
      match: MPIN
      label: It asks for
      finding: A web page asking for your MPIN is a phishing page. Enter it only
        inside the official app.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: mpin
      part: 
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Address
      finding: paylite-merchant-pay.example is a separate domain, not the official
        app.
      linkAddress: paylite-merchant-pay.example/canteen
      clueId: domain
      part: 
    evidence:
    - id: sticker
      title: QR sticker over the real one
      whyItMatters: Tampered QR codes send you to fake pages.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: mpin
      title: Asks for your MPIN on a web page
      whyItMatters: Your MPIN belongs only in the app.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: domain
      title: Unofficial domain
      whyItMatters: Not PayLite's own app or site.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Scam! A sticker, a web page and an MPIN request. Real payments happen
      <b>inside the app</b>.
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 17:
      Real-World Applications and Case Studies"
    hint: Look closely at the QR code itself, and think about where a real payment
      happens.
    activity:
      kind: 0
      title: 
      instruction: 
      items: []
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: H10
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: You find a fake school login page. Its admin page is publicly open and
      shows a list of <b>student passwords</b> it has captured. <b>What is the ethical
      action?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'Using stolen passwords, even to help, is not allowed and can hurt
      the victims more. Posting the link sends more people to the fake page, and
      testing it gives away your own password. Report it: IT has the right to act.
      (ACM Code of Ethics: avoid harm, respect privacy, use only what you''re allowed
      to.)'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: addr
      field: 5
      match: student-portal-login.example
      label: The address
      finding: For your report, write it in a non-clickable form, e.g. hxxp://student-portal-login[.]example,
        so nobody opens it by accident.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: report
      part: addr
    - id: open
      field: 5
      match: No sign-in needed
      label: Open access
      finding: Being able to see it doesn't make it yours to use. Using it further
        would be unauthorized access.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: unauth
      part: open
    - id: r1
      field: 5
      match: student_0417
      label: Captured entry
      finding: Real victims' stolen passwords. Handling them breaks their privacy
        - even to help.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: privacy
      part: r1
    - id: r2
      field: 5
      match: student_0932
      label: Captured entry
      finding: Another victim. Their data isn't yours to download or share.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: privacy
      part: r2
    - id: download
      field: 5
      match: DOWNLOAD LIST
      label: Download
      finding: 'Downloading means handling stolen data without authorization. (Simulated:
        nothing downloads.)'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: unauth
      part: download
    evidence:
    - id: unauth
      title: No authorization
      whyItMatters: Access computing resources only when authorized.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: privacy
      title: Other people's data
      whyItMatters: Respect privacy; avoid harm.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: report
      title: Report it safely
      whyItMatters: Screenshots and a non-clickable URL for IT.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Download the list and message each victim yourself
    - Post the link on the freedom wall to warn everyone
    - Test it with your own real password to confirm it works
    - Report it to the school's IT office with screenshots and the web address, without
      logging in or using the stolen data
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: Report it, don't touch it. Good intentions don't make <b>using stolen
      data</b> okay.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105) \u2014 Week 16: Professional
      Organization and ACM Code of Ethics"
    hint: Which choice helps the victims without you touching the stolen data?
    activity:
      kind: 2
      title: "Admin panel  \xB7  fake school login"
      instruction: Look at the page, but don't touch anything. What is the right
        thing to do?
      items:
      - key: addr
        role: 0
        label: Address
        text: http://student-portal-login.example/admin
      - key: open
        role: 8
        label: 
        text: No sign-in needed to view this page.
      - key: r1
        role: 6
        label: captured
        text: "student_0417  \xB7  \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022"
      - key: r2
        role: 6
        label: captured
        text: "student_0932  \xB7  \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022"
      - key: hidden
        role: 8
        label: 
        text: Passwords are hidden in this simulation.
      - key: download
        role: 7
        label: 
        text: DOWNLOAD LIST
      minClues: 2
      outcomes:
      - You're now holding other people's stolen data without authorization, and
        contacting victims yourself can cause more harm.
      - The post sends more students to the fake page, and more passwords are captured.
      - Your own password joins the captured list.
      - IT takes action with the authority to protect the victims, and you never
        touched the stolen data.
  inboxExtras:
  - id: l5_old_welcome
    channel: 1
    sender: MacLab Coordinator
    senderDetail: labs@uphsl-binan.example
